<p>Most homeowners choosing kitchen cabinets end up weighing two routes: cabinets made to measure for your kitchen, or ready-made (modular, standard-size) cabinets assembled to fit. Neither is always better. This guide sets out the general differences so you can decide based on your kitchen and your priorities.</p>

<h2>The main differences</h2>
<div class="guide-table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Custom-made</th><th>Ready-made / modular</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fit</td><td>Built to your measured dimensions, so it can handle uneven walls, bulkheads and awkward corners.</td><td>Standard widths; gaps are usually filled with filler panels, which works best in regular spaces.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Layout and storage</td><td>Drawers, pull-outs, corner solutions and heights can be specified for how you cook.</td><td>Choose from the sizes and fittings in the range.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Materials and finishes</td><td>Wider choice of board, finish and colour, depending on the maker.</td><td>Limited to the range offered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lead time</td><td>Usually longer, because cabinets are measured, designed, produced and installed for your kitchen.</td><td>Often shorter if items are in stock.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Depends on size, materials and fittings; get a written quotation.</td><td>Often lower upfront for simple layouts, but extras and fillers can add up. Compare like for like.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Installation</td><td>Normally done by the maker's team after a site measurement.</td><td>May be assembled by you, a handyman or the seller; confirm who is responsible for the result.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>When custom tends to suit better</h2>
<ul>
<li>Your kitchen has unusual dimensions, pillars, bulkheads or sloped walls.</li>
<li>You want specific storage, such as tall pull-out larders or a particular appliance layout.</li>
<li>You care about a continuous, built-in look to the ceiling.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When ready-made may be enough</h2>
<ul>
<li>The kitchen is a regular rectangle with straight walls.</li>
<li>You need cabinets quickly or are working to a tight, simple specification.</li>
<li>You are comfortable with standard sizes and a limited colour range.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A simple checklist before you decide</h2>
<ol>
<li>Measure your kitchen (see <a href="/calculating-kitchen-cabinet-space/">our measuring guide</a>) and note the pillars, windows, pipes and appliances.</li>
<li>Write down what you must store and how you cook, so drawers and shelves match real use.</li>
<li>Ask for written quotations that list materials, fittings, installation and warranty, then compare them line by line. Our <a href="/kitchen-cabinet-materials-malaysia/">materials guide</a> lists useful questions.</li>
<li>Confirm lead time, who installs, and what happens if something does not fit.</li>
</ol>

